Tamarin Bay opens to the west, where the Tamarin river meets the sea. The public beach looks out over the bay, with the mountains of the west rising inland — the setting for some of the most beautiful sunsets on the coast.

The bay is also a piece of surfing history. Once called Santosha Bay, it became famous after the 1974 film *Forgotten Island of Santosha*, and its two breaks, "Dal" to the south and "Black Stone" to the north, are still surfed today, mostly by experienced locals.

In the early morning, spinner and bottlenose dolphins are often seen in the bay before they return to the open sea.

## Good to know

- The river mouth can make the water cloudy after heavy rain.
- Surf only within your level, and respect the local line-up.
